WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.354 --> 00:00:07.758
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.087
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.087 --> 00:00:12.644
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.646 --> 00:00:15.129
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:17.519
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.521 --> 00:00:19.716
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.718 --> 00:00:21.384
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.795 --> 00:00:24.131
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:26.226
we are going to be talking

00:00:26.422 --> 00:00:28.658
about the history of feminism.

00:00:29.265 --> 00:00:31.428
It is the story of a very

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.197
simple idea: the idea that women

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:36.532
are just as valuable as men.

00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:40.352
And it is the story of the arguments,

00:00:40.354 --> 00:00:43.446
protests, hunger strikes and daily

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:46.107
courage it took for that idea to be

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:48.613
accepted, and of the places where

00:00:48.613 --> 00:00:51.265
the argument is still going on.

00:00:51.582 --> 00:00:53.904
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:53.906 --> 00:00:56.493
minute and get right into it.

00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:01.706
Nearly 3,000 years ago, a Greek poet told

00:01:01.708 --> 00:01:05.478
the story of a king who couldn't get home.

00:01:06.273 --> 00:01:08.029
You probably know the story.

00:01:08.524 --> 00:01:10.942
Perhaps you've even seen the recent

00:01:10.944 --> 00:01:12.946
Hollywood attempt to tell it.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.270
The king was Odysseus, and

00:01:16.272 --> 00:01:18.578
the poem was the Odyssey.

00:01:19.861 --> 00:01:22.955
After the Trojan War, Odysseus spent ten

00:01:22.957 --> 00:01:25.972
years trying to sail back to the island

00:01:25.972 --> 00:01:29.339
of Ithaca, and while he was away, his

00:01:29.341 --> 00:01:32.798
palace filled up with unwanted guests.

00:01:33.264 --> 00:01:35.819
They were men who wanted to marry his

00:01:35.821 --> 00:01:38.305
wife, Penelope, and take his throne.

00:01:38.955 --> 00:01:41.509
They ate his food, drank his wine,

00:01:41.511 --> 00:01:44.363
and made themselves very much at home.

00:01:45.836 --> 00:01:48.810
Early in the poem, there is a scene that

00:01:48.812 --> 00:01:51.784
doesn't make it into the movie versions.

00:01:52.847 --> 00:01:55.768
A singer was entertaining the guests in

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.913
the great hall with a sad song about Greek

00:01:58.913 --> 00:02:01.089
heroes trying to get home from the war.

00:02:02.225 --> 00:02:04.946
Penelope heard it from her room

00:02:04.948 --> 00:02:07.316
upstairs, and it upset her.

00:02:07.891 --> 00:02:09.913
So she came down and asked

00:02:09.915 --> 00:02:12.012
him to sing something else.

00:02:13.691 --> 00:02:16.359
Before the singer could answer, her

00:02:16.361 --> 00:02:18.799
son, Telemachus, who was still a

00:02:18.799 --> 00:02:21.987
teenager, told her to go back upstairs.

00:02:22.576 --> 00:02:24.356
"Go back into your own quarters,"

00:02:24.358 --> 00:02:25.975
he said, "and take up your own

00:02:25.985 --> 00:02:28.330
work, the loom and the distaff."

00:02:29.226 --> 00:02:32.327
A loom, by the way, is a frame for

00:02:32.329 --> 00:02:35.154
weaving cloth, and a distaff is

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:37.880
a stick used for spinning wool.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.403
In other words, go and do women's work.

00:02:43.382 --> 00:02:45.994
And then, as if that wasn't enough, he

00:02:45.996 --> 00:02:48.606
added: "Speech will be the business of

00:02:48.617 --> 00:02:51.691
men, all men, and of me most of all; for

00:02:51.693 --> 00:02:54.534
mine is the power in this household."

00:02:56.113 --> 00:02:58.035
Penelope did as she was told.

00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:00.396
She went back to her room.

00:03:01.516 --> 00:03:04.441
The classical historian Mary Beard has

00:03:04.443 --> 00:03:07.366
called this the first recorded example

00:03:07.571 --> 00:03:10.781
of a man telling a woman to shut up.

00:03:11.852 --> 00:03:14.448
Now, Telemachus has not gone down

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.281
in history as a famous chauvinist or

00:03:17.626 --> 00:03:20.556
anti-female character; he was just

00:03:20.558 --> 00:03:23.314
doing what men did, and for most

00:03:23.314 --> 00:03:25.760
of history, that was to make women

00:03:25.762 --> 00:03:28.350
know that they were inferior to men.

00:03:30.044 --> 00:03:32.518
Aristotle, the most influential

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.631
philosopher who ever lived, taught that

00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:38.859
a woman was something like an incomplete

00:03:38.861 --> 00:03:41.439
man, naturally made to be ruled.

00:03:42.446 --> 00:03:45.077
And of course religious authorities

00:03:45.079 --> 00:03:47.482
pointed to Eve, the first woman,

00:03:47.934 --> 00:03:50.326
who ate the forbidden fruit;

00:03:50.328 --> 00:03:53.145
womankind, the argument went, had

00:03:53.145 --> 00:03:56.043
been the source of sin ever since.

00:03:57.185 --> 00:03:59.537
Biologically, too, women's bodies

00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:01.890
were believed to work differently

00:04:01.891 --> 00:04:04.175
to men's, in a way that limited

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:06.165
a woman's ability to think.

00:04:07.334 --> 00:04:10.593
For centuries, doctors taught that women's

00:04:10.595 --> 00:04:13.620
bodies and minds were governed by their

00:04:13.623 --> 00:04:16.668
wombs, and that too much excitement, or

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.870
later, too much education, was dangerous.

00:04:21.353 --> 00:04:24.273
Even as recently as the 1870s, a

00:04:24.275 --> 00:04:27.559
Harvard professor wrote a successful

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.252
book warning that serious study

00:04:30.254 --> 00:04:33.268
would damage a young woman's health

00:04:33.542 --> 00:04:35.669
and her ability to have children.

00:04:36.422 --> 00:04:39.594
Study, he wrote, was dangerous for women.

00:04:40.125 --> 00:04:41.964
But, of course, it was recommended

00:04:41.966 --> 00:04:44.019
for men, whose bodies could handle it.

00:04:45.414 --> 00:04:47.575
And when argument was not

00:04:47.577 --> 00:04:49.564
enough, there was fear.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.153
Between roughly 1500 and 1700, tens

00:04:54.155 --> 00:04:57.839
of thousands of people were executed

00:04:57.852 --> 00:05:00.267
in Europe as witches, and the

00:05:00.269 --> 00:05:03.099
great majority of them were women,

00:05:03.518 --> 00:05:06.185
disproportionately the old, poor,

00:05:06.187 --> 00:05:08.932
the widowed, and the inconvenient.

00:05:09.844 --> 00:05:12.372
No doubt you know about this already,

00:05:12.374 --> 00:05:14.763
but if you are thirsty for more, we

00:05:14.764 --> 00:05:17.731
made a whole episode about the history

00:05:17.733 --> 00:05:20.151
of witches; that's episode 312.

00:05:21.305 --> 00:05:23.820
This was simply how the world

00:05:23.822 --> 00:05:26.769
worked, or was understood to work.

00:05:27.162 --> 00:05:30.806
Men ruled supreme; the role of women was

00:05:30.808 --> 00:05:34.632
to produce children, boys ideally, and not

00:05:34.643 --> 00:05:37.030
to get too involved in anything else, in

00:05:37.032 --> 00:05:39.537
case they hurt themselves or someone else.

00:05:40.866 --> 00:05:43.601
And given that half of the world's

00:05:43.603 --> 00:05:46.416
population is female, it is hard to

00:05:46.416 --> 00:05:49.254
believe today that this was so widely

00:05:49.256 --> 00:05:52.092
accepted, and it makes the people who

00:05:52.093 --> 00:05:55.290
first challenged it even more remarkable.

00:05:56.492 --> 00:05:58.707
Before we meet them, I need to give

00:05:58.709 --> 00:06:00.796
you one more piece of background.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:04.851
The word "feminism" is surprisingly new.

00:06:05.643 --> 00:06:08.867
It came from the French féminisme, and

00:06:08.869 --> 00:06:12.176
only settled into English in the 1890s.

00:06:13.136 --> 00:06:15.799
For most of this story, the people

00:06:15.801 --> 00:06:18.070
fighting these battles had no

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.556
name for what they were doing.

00:06:19.942 --> 00:06:22.185
They simply thought of it as justice.

00:06:23.192 --> 00:06:25.224
So, who were they?

00:06:25.647 --> 00:06:28.507
Well, in 1792, in London, a

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:32.109
self-educated published named Mary

00:06:32.353 --> 00:06:35.213
Wollstonecraft published a book called.

00:06:35.622 --> 00:06:38.314
A Vindication of the Rights of Women.

00:06:39.694 --> 00:06:41.589
Like every women of her time,

00:06:41.591 --> 00:06:44.334
Wollstonecraft couldn't sit in Parliament,

00:06:44.623 --> 00:06:47.414
study at a university or practise law.

00:06:48.314 --> 00:06:50.035
What she could do was write.

00:06:51.675 --> 00:06:54.025
Revolutionary France France had

00:06:54.027 --> 00:06:56.755
just declared the rights of man, and

00:06:56.755 --> 00:06:59.248
Wollstonecraft asked the obvious question:

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.445
What about the other half of humanity

00:07:02.974 --> 00:07:05.027
women, she wrote, were not

00:07:05.029 --> 00:07:06.922
naturally silly or weak.

00:07:07.279 --> 00:07:09.538
They appeared so because they

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.720
were denied an education and

00:07:11.721 --> 00:07:13.919
then mocked for not having one.

00:07:14.745 --> 00:07:17.258
If you teach a girl only to be pretty

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:19.431
and pleasing, don't be surprised

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.263
when that is all she becomes.

00:07:22.539 --> 00:07:24.156
And she was careful to say

00:07:24.158 --> 00:07:25.648
exactly what she wanted.

00:07:26.417 --> 00:07:29.117
"I do not wish women to have power over

00:07:29.119 --> 00:07:31.817
men," she wrote, "but over themselves."

00:07:33.221 --> 00:07:35.353
Wollstonecraft died young, at

00:07:35.355 --> 00:07:37.705
38, of an infection after giving

00:07:37.706 --> 00:07:39.809
birth, which was yet another big

00:07:39.811 --> 00:07:41.715
risk simply of being a woman.

00:07:42.403 --> 00:07:45.287
Her daughter, by the way, was called Mary,

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:47.897
and she grew up to write Frankenstein.

00:07:49.092 --> 00:07:52.026
Now, for decades, Wollstonecraft's

00:07:52.028 --> 00:07:54.356
book was ahead of its time.

00:07:54.817 --> 00:07:57.479
But by the middle of the 19th

00:07:57.481 --> 00:08:00.324
century, the argument had found

00:08:00.338 --> 00:08:02.502
a somewhat organised voice.

00:08:03.691 --> 00:08:06.403
In 1840, two American women,

00:08:06.405 --> 00:08:09.794
Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia

00:08:09.809 --> 00:08:12.331
Mott, travelled to London for a

00:08:12.333 --> 00:08:15.015
world conference against slavery.

00:08:15.964 --> 00:08:18.086
When they arrived, they were told

00:08:18.088 --> 00:08:20.207
that, as women, they couldn't sit

00:08:20.402 --> 00:08:22.375
with the other delegates or speak.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.421
They were sent to a separate section

00:08:25.423 --> 00:08:28.345
and made to watch from behind a curtain.

00:08:29.242 --> 00:08:32.258
The two of them got talking, and

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.275
eight years later, in 1848, they

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:37.306
organised a meeting of their own.

00:08:38.557 --> 00:08:41.770
Around 300 people gathered in a chapel

00:08:41.772 --> 00:08:44.899
in the small town of Seneca Falls, in

00:08:45.141 --> 00:08:48.111
New York State, for the first convention

00:08:48.113 --> 00:08:50.562
ever held on the rights of women.

00:08:51.837 --> 00:08:54.765
They issued a declaration modelled, line

00:08:54.767 --> 00:08:57.401
by line, on the American Declaration

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:59.808
of Independence, with one addition.

00:09:00.914 --> 00:09:03.409
All men and women, it

00:09:03.411 --> 00:09:06.261
said, are created equal.

00:09:07.998 --> 00:09:11.324
Their list of demands, to modern Western

00:09:11.326 --> 00:09:14.566
ears, is painfully modest: the right to

00:09:14.568 --> 00:09:16.816
own property, to keep their own wages,

00:09:16.818 --> 00:09:19.182
to go to university, to speak in public,

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:22.689
and, most controversially of all, to vote.

00:09:24.217 --> 00:09:27.268
Even at Seneca Falls, the demand

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.605
for the vote nearly failed to pass.

00:09:31.876 --> 00:09:34.176
This was the beginning of what

00:09:34.178 --> 00:09:36.782
historians now call the first wave

00:09:36.795 --> 00:09:39.377
of feminism, a movement with one

00:09:39.379 --> 00:09:42.202
great goal at its centre: the vote.

00:09:43.453 --> 00:09:46.480
Now, to understand why these demands

00:09:46.482 --> 00:09:49.844
sounded so radical, you have to remember

00:09:50.127 --> 00:09:52.514
how the law treated women at the time.

00:09:52.887 --> 00:09:54.724
They really were second-class

00:09:54.726 --> 00:09:56.752
citizens in the eyes of the law.

00:09:57.479 --> 00:10:00.335
In England, a married woman had no

00:10:00.337 --> 00:10:03.023
separate legal existence at all.

00:10:04.034 --> 00:10:07.214
As one legal scholar put it: husband

00:10:07.216 --> 00:10:09.953
and wife are one person in law,

00:10:10.337 --> 00:10:12.065
and that person is the husband.

00:10:13.612 --> 00:10:15.852
In practice, this meant that on

00:10:15.854 --> 00:10:18.381
her wedding day, a woman's property

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:20.164
became her husband's property.

00:10:20.862 --> 00:10:24.315
Her wages, if she earned any, were his.

00:10:24.804 --> 00:10:26.717
She couldn't sign a contract, and

00:10:26.719 --> 00:10:28.456
if the marriage collapsed, the

00:10:28.456 --> 00:10:31.201
children were legally his too.

00:10:32.568 --> 00:10:35.698
It took until 1870, and really until

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:39.175
1882, for British wives to win the right

00:10:39.324 --> 00:10:41.372
to keep their own property and earnings.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:45.094
And although it's easy to see men

00:10:45.096 --> 00:10:47.475
as the universal villains here,

00:10:47.648 --> 00:10:49.989
united in shutting women out of

00:10:49.991 --> 00:10:52.708
society, that's not completely true.

00:10:53.606 --> 00:10:56.630
The cause had some notable male allies.

00:10:57.622 --> 00:11:00.190
In 1866, the philosopher John

00:11:00.192 --> 00:11:03.201
Stuart Mill, who was then a member

00:11:03.203 --> 00:11:05.865
of Parliament, presented the first

00:11:05.867 --> 00:11:08.370
mass petition for women's votes.

00:11:09.232 --> 00:11:11.822
The following year, he proposed

00:11:11.824 --> 00:11:13.994
changing the word "man" to

00:11:14.148 --> 00:11:16.501
"person" in a new voting law.

00:11:17.407 --> 00:11:20.211
He was laughed at in the House of Commons.

00:11:20.818 --> 00:11:21.741
Women voting?

00:11:22.146 --> 00:11:23.530
It was a ridiculous idea.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:25.320
They couldn't be trusted

00:11:25.322 --> 00:11:26.814
with something so important.

00:11:28.291 --> 00:11:31.378
And some of the fiercest opposition

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.760
came from women themselves.

00:11:34.772 --> 00:11:37.938
In 1870, Queen Victoria, who was at the

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.103
time almost certainly the most powerful

00:11:41.103 --> 00:11:43.530
woman in the world, wrote a private

00:11:43.532 --> 00:11:46.234
letter asking anyone who could speak or

00:11:46.234 --> 00:11:49.737
write to help stop what she called "this

00:11:49.739 --> 00:11:53.152
mad, wicked folly of 'Woman's Rights'".

00:11:54.605 --> 00:11:57.433
And in 1908, Mary Ward, who was one

00:11:57.435 --> 00:12:00.019
of the best-selling novelists in

00:12:00.028 --> 00:12:02.972
Britain, helped set up the Women's

00:12:02.974 --> 00:12:05.570
National Anti-Suffrage League.

00:12:06.219 --> 00:12:09.100
It argued that women had their own world,

00:12:09.102 --> 00:12:11.699
the home, the family and charity, and

00:12:11.699 --> 00:12:13.731
that politics should be left to men.

00:12:14.985 --> 00:12:17.917
Progress, when it came, came first at

00:12:17.919 --> 00:12:21.086
the edges of the English-speaking world.

00:12:22.527 --> 00:12:25.394
In New Zealand, a campaigner named

00:12:25.396 --> 00:12:28.599
Kate Sheppard organised petition after

00:12:28.599 --> 00:12:31.005
petition, each bigger than the last.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.612
The final one, in 1893, carried

00:12:34.614 --> 00:12:37.828
the signatures of nearly a quarter

00:12:38.049 --> 00:12:40.724
of all adult women in the country.

00:12:42.135 --> 00:12:44.495
The sheets were glued together

00:12:44.497 --> 00:12:47.092
in a single roll, and when it was

00:12:47.116 --> 00:12:49.719
presented to parliament, a supportive

00:12:49.721 --> 00:12:51.900
politician unrolled it down the

00:12:51.913 --> 00:12:54.144
central aisle of the chamber until

00:12:54.146 --> 00:12:56.310
it hit the far wall with a thump.

00:12:57.303 --> 00:13:00.535
It was 270 metres long.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:04.348
It sounds like a bit of a gimmick, the

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.849
sort of thing that might be done today to

00:13:06.851 --> 00:13:10.734
go viral on social media, but it worked.

00:13:11.739 --> 00:13:14.297
That same year, New Zealand became

00:13:14.299 --> 00:13:17.156
the first self-governing country where

00:13:17.157 --> 00:13:20.069
women could vote in national elections.

00:13:21.014 --> 00:13:23.505
Australia followed in 1902, though

00:13:23.507 --> 00:13:25.996
not for Indigenous women, who were

00:13:26.196 --> 00:13:28.071
second-class citizens among the

00:13:28.073 --> 00:13:29.886
already second-class citizens.

00:13:31.177 --> 00:13:33.796
Britain took longer and, as you

00:13:33.798 --> 00:13:36.668
may know, the fight turned bitter.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.774
For decades, British campaigners had

00:13:40.776 --> 00:13:43.869
asked politely: petitions, meetings,

00:13:44.071 --> 00:13:45.363
letters to members of Parliament.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.540
They were known as the suffragists,

00:13:48.542 --> 00:13:51.021
from "suffrage", the right to vote.

00:13:51.813 --> 00:13:54.826
And they were, on the whole, ignored.

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:59.439
So in 1903, in Manchester, Emmeline

00:13:59.441 --> 00:14:01.914
Pankhurst and her daughters

00:14:02.183 --> 00:14:04.140
set up a new organisation.

00:14:05.059 --> 00:14:08.030
If nobody was listening to their words,

00:14:08.032 --> 00:14:10.620
they would try something else, and

00:14:10.819 --> 00:14:14.170
their motto was: deeds, not words.

00:14:15.525 --> 00:14:18.716
The newspapers called them "suffragettes".

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:21.175
They interrupted political

00:14:21.177 --> 00:14:23.234
meetings, smashed shop windows

00:14:23.369 --> 00:14:25.984
and set fire to empty buildings.

00:14:26.767 --> 00:14:29.387
In prison, they went on hunger strike,

00:14:29.389 --> 00:14:31.524
and the government responded by

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:34.592
force-feeding them through rubber tubes.

00:14:35.289 --> 00:14:37.851
In 1913, a suffragette called

00:14:37.853 --> 00:14:40.413
Emily Davison was killed when

00:14:40.413 --> 00:14:41.887
she stepped in front of the

00:14:41.889 --> 00:14:43.634
king's horse at the Epsom Derby.

00:14:44.879 --> 00:14:47.202
We actually did an entire episode on

00:14:47.204 --> 00:14:49.396
the suffragettes back in the early

00:14:49.396 --> 00:14:51.576
days of this show; it's episode

00:14:51.578 --> 00:14:54.249
number 36, if you want the full story.

00:14:55.776 --> 00:14:59.075
Then the First World War came, and with

00:14:59.077 --> 00:15:02.205
the men at the front, women went into

00:15:02.205 --> 00:15:05.076
the factories, the fields, the hospitals

00:15:05.078 --> 00:15:07.660
and the offices, doing the jobs that

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:10.185
they had long been told were beyond them.

00:15:11.736 --> 00:15:14.579
Sure enough, in 1918, British women

00:15:14.581 --> 00:15:17.991
finally got the vote, but not all of them.

00:15:18.574 --> 00:15:21.377
You had to be over 30, and you, or your

00:15:21.379 --> 00:15:23.892
husband, had to own or rent a home.

00:15:24.767 --> 00:15:27.682
Men, meanwhile, could vote at 21.

00:15:29.055 --> 00:15:31.667
Ten years later, in 1928, women

00:15:31.669 --> 00:15:34.363
got it on the same terms as men.

00:15:35.693 --> 00:15:37.255
American women got the

00:15:37.257 --> 00:15:38.958
vote nationally in 1920.

00:15:39.353 --> 00:15:42.405
Turkey gave women the vote in 1934.

00:15:42.779 --> 00:15:45.623
France, for all of its revolutionary

00:15:45.625 --> 00:15:48.151
rhetoric, waited until 1944, and

00:15:48.151 --> 00:15:51.820
Switzerland, remarkably, until 1971.

00:15:52.618 --> 00:15:55.010
So, the vote was won.

00:15:55.636 --> 00:15:57.369
And then something interesting

00:15:57.371 --> 00:15:58.929
happened, which is that not

00:15:58.937 --> 00:16:00.412
very much happened at all.

00:16:01.369 --> 00:16:04.521
Women could vote, but a woman in

00:16:04.523 --> 00:16:07.869
1950s Britain or America often had

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:10.041
trouble opening a bank account,

00:16:10.043 --> 00:16:12.481
signing a rental contract or taking

00:16:12.481 --> 00:16:15.159
out a loan without a man's signature.

00:16:16.122 --> 00:16:18.700
Universities and professions

00:16:18.702 --> 00:16:21.093
had quotas or simple bans.

00:16:21.767 --> 00:16:24.148
A husband controlled the household,

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:26.529
and what happened inside a marriage

00:16:26.781 --> 00:16:29.246
was considered nobody else's business.

00:16:30.094 --> 00:16:32.588
There had been progress, sure, but

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:35.008
even in the most equal societies,

00:16:35.298 --> 00:16:37.308
women were far from equal.

00:16:38.712 --> 00:16:41.776
In 1949, the French philosopher Simone

00:16:41.778 --> 00:16:44.357
de Beauvoir published The Second

00:16:44.373 --> 00:16:47.506
Sex, and famously wrote: "One is not

00:16:47.508 --> 00:16:50.552
born, but rather becomes, a woman."

00:16:51.761 --> 00:16:54.756
What she meant was that girls weren't

00:16:54.758 --> 00:16:57.671
born inferior, but they were trained

00:16:57.988 --> 00:17:00.442
and shaped into a smaller life.

00:17:01.763 --> 00:17:04.550
The book scandalised respectable opinion.

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:07.414
The Vatican put it on its official

00:17:07.416 --> 00:17:09.561
list of forbidden books, which,

00:17:09.845 --> 00:17:11.931
as so often happens when a book is

00:17:11.933 --> 00:17:14.018
banned, did wonders for its sales.

00:17:14.987 --> 00:17:17.883
Then, fourteen years later, an American

00:17:17.885 --> 00:17:20.557
journalist named Betty Friedan wrote

00:17:20.613 --> 00:17:23.137
about the unhappiness of the suburban

00:17:23.139 --> 00:17:25.661
housewife, surrounded by every modern

00:17:25.663 --> 00:17:27.965
comfort and secretly desperate.

00:17:29.159 --> 00:17:30.505
She called it "the

00:17:30.507 --> 00:17:32.449
problem that has no name".

00:17:33.676 --> 00:17:36.412
Her book sold in the millions, because

00:17:36.414 --> 00:17:39.437
millions of women recognised themselves in

00:17:39.439 --> 00:17:42.835
it, and now the problem did have a name.

00:17:43.987 --> 00:17:46.433
This was the second wave: a

00:17:46.435 --> 00:17:48.970
movement no longer about the

00:17:49.008 --> 00:17:52.140
ballot box, but about pay, work,

00:17:52.142 --> 00:17:55.370
education, the body and the home.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:57.972
Its battles were fought in

00:17:57.974 --> 00:17:59.708
places you might not expect.

00:18:00.385 --> 00:18:04.196
In 1968, at the Ford car factory in

00:18:04.198 --> 00:18:08.007
Dagenham, in east London, 187 women

00:18:08.218 --> 00:18:10.598
who sewed car seat covers walked

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:13.126
out when their work was officially

00:18:13.127 --> 00:18:16.068
graded as less skilled than the men's.

00:18:17.142 --> 00:18:19.537
Their strike stopped car production

00:18:19.539 --> 00:18:22.206
entirely, dragged a government minister

00:18:22.476 --> 00:18:26.126
to the negotiating table, and led directly

00:18:26.128 --> 00:18:29.167
to Britain's Equal Pay Act of 1970.

00:18:30.515 --> 00:18:33.646
And on the 24th of October,

00:18:33.648 --> 00:18:37.009
1975, in Iceland, around nine

00:18:37.346 --> 00:18:40.004
out of ten women simply stopped.

00:18:40.717 --> 00:18:42.475
They didn't go to work, and

00:18:42.477 --> 00:18:44.168
they didn't cook, clean or

00:18:44.342 --> 00:18:45.738
look after the children either.

00:18:46.283 --> 00:18:47.862
They called it a "day off".

00:18:48.746 --> 00:18:51.473
Fish factories closed, and fathers turned

00:18:51.475 --> 00:18:53.934
up at the office with their children,

00:18:54.305 --> 00:18:56.495
so their bosses had to go out and buy

00:18:56.497 --> 00:18:58.685
sweets and pencils to keep them busy.

00:18:59.459 --> 00:19:01.963
Sausages reportedly sold out across

00:19:01.965 --> 00:19:04.610
the country, as fathers realised they

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:06.202
didn't know how to cook anything else.

00:19:07.581 --> 00:19:09.923
Five years later, Iceland elected

00:19:09.925 --> 00:19:12.477
not just its first female president,

00:19:12.673 --> 00:19:14.996
but the first woman in the world

00:19:14.998 --> 00:19:17.391
to be democratically elected head

00:19:17.394 --> 00:19:20.590
of state: Vigdís Finnbogadóttir.

00:19:21.944 --> 00:19:24.679
The same period brought the contraceptive

00:19:24.681 --> 00:19:27.414
pill, which for the first time in history

00:19:27.704 --> 00:19:31.040
gave women reliable control over when, and

00:19:31.042 --> 00:19:34.297
whether, to have children, and so control

00:19:34.311 --> 00:19:36.593
over their education and their careers.

00:19:38.014 --> 00:19:40.516
Meanwhile, the legal scaffolding of

00:19:40.518 --> 00:19:43.303
the old world came down piece by piece.

00:19:44.268 --> 00:19:46.966
Britain outlawed sex discrimination

00:19:46.968 --> 00:19:49.432
in 1975, the same year it became

00:19:49.461 --> 00:19:51.856
illegal to refuse a woman a mortgage

00:19:51.858 --> 00:19:53.918
simply because she was a woman.

00:19:55.136 --> 00:19:57.975
Bizarrely enough, it took until

00:19:57.977 --> 00:20:00.630
1991 for rape within marriage

00:20:00.824 --> 00:20:02.296
to become a crime in England.

00:20:03.553 --> 00:20:06.401
Now, so far this story has largely

00:20:06.403 --> 00:20:09.668
stayed in Europe and North America, but

00:20:09.928 --> 00:20:12.032
the argument was going on everywhere.

00:20:13.597 --> 00:20:15.865
The world's first female prime

00:20:15.867 --> 00:20:18.511
minister was Sirimavo Bandaranaike,

00:20:18.839 --> 00:20:21.846
who took office in Sri Lanka in 1960.

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:25.394
India followed in 1966,

00:20:25.396 --> 00:20:27.718
and Israel in 1969.

00:20:28.925 --> 00:20:31.721
Almost certainly the most important, and

00:20:31.723 --> 00:20:34.307
most divisive, British prime minister

00:20:34.322 --> 00:20:37.553
of recent times was a woman: Margaret

00:20:37.555 --> 00:20:40.696
Thatcher, who came to power in 1979.

00:20:42.064 --> 00:20:45.370
As of the time of recording, 63 of the

00:20:45.372 --> 00:20:49.025
193 member countries of the United Nations

00:20:49.404 --> 00:20:51.526
have had a woman leading the government.

00:20:52.356 --> 00:20:54.262
That's only a third, and there's

00:20:54.264 --> 00:20:55.870
certainly a long way to go,

00:20:56.259 --> 00:20:58.627
but it is still some progress.

00:20:59.875 --> 00:21:01.990
But this history doesn't

00:21:01.992 --> 00:21:04.369
only move in one direction.

00:21:05.525 --> 00:21:08.655
Iranian women got the vote in 1963.

00:21:09.559 --> 00:21:12.591
And in the 1970s, women in Tehran

00:21:12.593 --> 00:21:15.255
and Kabul went to university,

00:21:15.576 --> 00:21:17.819
worked as doctors and judges, and

00:21:17.821 --> 00:21:20.197
dressed more or less as they liked.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:24.598
But after Iran's revolution in 1979,

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.637
the headscarf became compulsory,

00:21:27.889 --> 00:21:30.021
women judges were removed from

00:21:30.023 --> 00:21:32.296
their posts, and the persecution

00:21:32.319 --> 00:21:34.754
of women continues to this day.

00:21:35.812 --> 00:21:38.301
In Afghanistan, when the Taliban

00:21:38.303 --> 00:21:40.323
returned to power in 2021,

00:21:40.773 --> 00:21:42.949
girls were banned from secondary

00:21:42.951 --> 00:21:45.261
school, and women from university.

00:21:46.225 --> 00:21:48.485
In August 2024, the Taliban

00:21:48.487 --> 00:21:51.163
published a new law on morality.

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:54.584
Among many other rules, it said that

00:21:54.586 --> 00:21:57.048
a woman's voice should not be heard

00:21:57.048 --> 00:21:59.410
in public, whether she was singing,

00:21:59.412 --> 00:22:01.839
reciting or simply reading out loud.

00:22:03.107 --> 00:22:05.961
And even in countries that consistently

00:22:05.963 --> 00:22:08.229
rank highest in terms of gender

00:22:08.244 --> 00:22:11.411
equality, not everything is always equal.

00:22:12.247 --> 00:22:13.953
Salaries for men tend to be

00:22:13.955 --> 00:22:15.911
higher than salaries for women.

00:22:16.484 --> 00:22:19.250
Parliaments almost everywhere remain

00:22:19.252 --> 00:22:21.555
mostly male, as do boardrooms,

00:22:21.813 --> 00:22:24.031
and the unpaid work of raising

00:22:24.033 --> 00:22:26.322
children and caring for the old

00:22:26.695 --> 00:22:29.083
disproportionately falls on women.

00:22:30.376 --> 00:22:33.048
And there is, of course, the even

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:36.044
darker side: the abuse and harassment

00:22:36.193 --> 00:22:38.763
of women by men, as things like the

00:22:38.765 --> 00:22:41.333
Harvey Weinstein case and the MeToo

00:22:41.335 --> 00:22:43.468
movement made abundantly clear.

00:22:44.365 --> 00:22:46.742
And there is the awful fact that

00:22:46.744 --> 00:22:49.045
every year, around 50,000 women

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:51.649
and girls are killed by a partner

00:22:51.651 --> 00:22:54.159
or a member of their own family.

00:22:54.631 --> 00:22:56.655
That is one every ten minutes.

00:22:57.738 --> 00:23:00.412
Mary Beard, the historian who pointed

00:23:00.414 --> 00:23:02.580
us at Penelope at the start of

00:23:02.589 --> 00:23:05.067
the episode, has spent her career

00:23:05.069 --> 00:23:07.469
studying how women's voices have

00:23:07.469 --> 00:23:09.910
been dismissed since ancient times.

00:23:10.532 --> 00:23:13.137
Western history, she argues, is

00:23:13.139 --> 00:23:15.910
in a large part a very long story

00:23:16.115 --> 00:23:18.259
of men not listening to women.

00:23:18.856 --> 00:23:20.584
And sometimes, much worse.

00:23:21.665 --> 00:23:24.714
Fortunately, today, if a teenage boy is

00:23:24.716 --> 00:23:27.606
playing loud music downstairs and his

00:23:27.606 --> 00:23:30.598
mother asks him to switch it to something

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:33.152
else, she might get a groan of "oh,

00:23:33.172 --> 00:23:35.817
Mum", but one at least hopes that she

00:23:35.819 --> 00:23:38.677
won't be told to return to her knitting.

00:23:40.783 --> 00:23:42.779
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:23:42.781 --> 00:23:44.963
episode on the history of feminism.

00:23:45.334 --> 00:23:46.773
As always, I would love to know

00:23:46.775 --> 00:23:48.304
what you thought of this episode.

00:23:48.706 --> 00:23:50.576
When did women in your country

00:23:50.578 --> 00:23:52.322
get the vote, who fought for

00:23:52.324 --> 00:23:53.817
it, and what is the story?

00:23:54.067 --> 00:23:55.712
I would love to know, so let's

00:23:55.714 --> 00:23:57.247
get this discussion started.

00:23:57.504 --> 00:23:59.120
Let me know in the comments below,

00:23:59.122 --> 00:24:00.879
if you're listening to this somewhere

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:02.676
where you can comment, and for the

00:24:02.678 --> 00:24:04.631
members among you, you can head right

00:24:04.648 --> 00:24:06.559
into our community forum, which is

00:24:06.561 --> 00:24:08.358
at community.leonardoenglish.com

00:24:08.666 --> 00:24:09.996
and get chatting away

00:24:09.998 --> 00:24:11.453
to other curious minds.

00:24:11.805 --> 00:24:14.144
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:14.146 --> 00:24:16.311
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:16.708 --> 00:24:19.142
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:19.144 --> 00:24:21.383
I'll catch you in the next episode.
